Specify accessible actions feedback and recoverable input errors (@computing-trust-accessible: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Check-authority-for-the-particular-object-and-effect-when-it-is-used.lingua#r_00250BA8CBCE73EF3045E82456") { r_007E1E3B2807ADC4AA74817156
A task contract must account for how a person perceives state and expresses an action. Start with the information and action meanings, then describe their realizations. In our proposed ledger interface, the object and amount are named explicitly, the submission control states the action, and a receipt distinguishes the submitted amount from the resulting total. An unlabeled icon or a color change alone does not communicate that contract to every participant.

Read the selected criteria in [WCAG 2.2 selected interaction criteria and conformance scope](../sources/WCAG-2.2-selected-interaction-criteria-and-conformance-scope.lingua#r_003FD04F1BEB6F767F948CC12E). WCAG 2.2 separates keyboard operation, focus, error identification, component semantics and status-message exposure; its conformance requirements concern full pages and complete processes. The common study below models only named actions and textual state. It cannot establish conformance of a rendered interface.

For an original interaction specification, use the following sequence. On the draft screen, expose the ledger name, an amount field with its accepted range, and review action. On review, repeat the exact object and amount and provide edit and submit actions. Submission freezes those arguments for the outstanding identity. Pending and unknown screens retain the operation description and offer retry of that same operation. Confirmation shows the receipt and makes clear that a new addition would be a new task. A temporary status update should be available through the chosen platform's accessible semantics without unexpectedly moving the person's position. Actual focus behavior must be specified and checked in the implementation.

Specify input and error paths too. Typing zero in a range of one through fifteen should identify the amount field and preserve the entered value for correction; silently replacing it with one changes intent. A person must be able to reach the relevant controls, determine the current position, activate them and leave the interaction through supported input methods. Information presented visually must have an appropriate accessible representation. This includes the uncertain outcome, not merely the happy-path receipt. Plain language and useful grouping matter alongside technical semantics; different people may need different presentation or assistance.

The finite program offers submit or retry as action identities with descriptive names and button roles. Its keyboard and pointer adapters perform the same abstract transition. That equality checks one design property. It does not test focus order, keyboard traps, screen-reader announcements, contrast, zoom, target sizes, cognitive load or hardware integration. The richer draft/review specification above is a proposed design, not implemented by that program.

**Work:** draw or write a complete interaction trace, including wrong input, correction, submission, lost reply and recovery. Explain the information available without relying on color or location. Describe actual device and assistive-technology checks needed before claiming support. Reuse [Connect assessment tasks to the claims their responses can support](../../inquiry/core/Connect-assessment-tasks-to-the-claims-their-responses-can-support.lingua#r_002B495CD7777FBC8C28058BF0) to distinguish a test's observable response from the wider claim it might be used to support.

**Continue:** [Check authority for the particular object and effect when it is used](Check-authority-for-the-particular-object-and-effect-when-it-is-used.lingua#r_00250BA8CBCE73EF3045E82456).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
